/**
\page port_admin The administrative interface to YARP ports

\tableofcontents

Most of the time, YARP ports are conduits for messages.
Users write messages to ports, and read messages from ports,
and the ports themselves don't inspect the content of those
messages.

In some cases, though, you may find yourself wishing to talk
to a port itself, in order to query it about its state, or to
request it to take some action (such as making or breaking a
connection).  There is a low level protocol for this, but it
is somewhat arcane (see \ref yarp_protocol_port).

To send an administrative message to a port is simple.
From the commandline, just use "yarp admin write" or
"yarp admin rpc" instead of "yarp write" or "yarp rpc":
\verbatim
  echo "listConnectionsIn" | yarp admin rpc /port_name
\endverbatim
This will list all the inputs to the port /port_name.

From code, set the "admin" flag in yarp::os::Network::write, or call
yarp::os::Port::setAdminMode.  Messages can be read/written as regular
yarp::os::Bottle objects.  Nothing else is needed.

Here are the things you can do using the administrative
interface:

- \ref port_admin_help
- \ref port_admin_add
- \ref port_admin_del
- \ref port_admin_list
- \ref port_admin_ver
- \ref port_admin_portmonitor
- \ref port_admin_portmonitor_params
- \ref port_admin_connection_params
- \ref port_admin_info
- \ref port_admin_qos

\section port_admin_help help

\verbatim
help
help $command
\endverbatim

\c help lists all the available administration commands, while
<tt>help $command</tt> gives the prototype and the description of the given
command (its parameters and its reply). For instance:
\verbatim
echo "help addConnection" | yarp admin rpc /port_name
\endverbatim

All the administration commands are defined in the thrift service
\c PortCoreMsgs (\c src/libYARP_os/src/idl/PortCoreMsgs.thrift), and the
descriptions given by \c help are the documentation comments of that file.


\section port_admin_add addConnection

\verbatim
addConnection /port/name
addConnection /port/name carrier
\endverbatim

Add an output to the specified port, using a given carrier (tcp, udp, etc.).
The carrier is optional.


\section port_admin_del delConnection

\verbatim
delConnection /port/name
\endverbatim

Remove any input or output connection involving the specified port.

The reply of both commands is <tt>ok success message</tt>, where
\c success is \c fail if the connection could not be added/removed, and
\c message is a human readable description of the result.
From code, use the corresponding methods of yarp::os::impl::PortCoreClient,
which return the structs yarp::os::return_addConnection and
yarp::os::return_delConnection. The field \c ret
of the returned struct is \c false if the remote port could not be reached.


\section port_admin_list listConnectionsIn / listConnectionsOut / getConnectionInfoIn / getConnectionInfoOut

\verbatim
listConnectionsIn
listConnectionsOut
getConnectionInfoIn  /portname
getConnectionInfoOut /portname
\endverbatim

\c listConnectionsIn and \c listConnectionsOut list the names of the ports
connected to the input or to the output of the port. The reply is
<tt>ok (names...)</tt>.

\c getConnectionInfoIn and \c getConnectionInfoOut give details about the
connection coming from (or going to) the specified port. The reply is
<tt>ret found from_port to_port carrier connectionless push</tt>, where
\c found is \c fail if there is no such connection.

From code, use the corresponding methods of yarp::os::impl::PortCoreClient,
which return the structs yarp::os::return_listConnections and
yarp::os::return_getConnectionInfo. The field \c ret of the returned struct
is \c false if the remote port could not be reached.


\section port_admin_ver getAdminProtocolVersion

\verbatim
getAdminProtocolVersion
\endverbatim

Report the version of the administrative protocol of the port. It is
distinct from the YARP library version. The reply is
<tt>ok major minor patch</tt>.

From code, use the corresponding method of yarp::os::impl::PortCoreClient,
which returns the struct yarp::os::return_getAdminProtocolVersion. The field
\c ret of the returned struct is \c false if the remote port could not be
reached.


\section port_admin_portmonitor attachPortmonitorIn / attachPortmonitorOut / detachPortmonitorIn / detachPortmonitorOut

\verbatim
attachPortmonitorIn  "(type dll) (file test_numbers1)"
attachPortmonitorOut "(type dll) (file test_numbers1)"
detachPortmonitorIn
detachPortmonitorOut
\endverbatim

Attach a portmonitor plug-in to the input or to the output of the port, or
detach it. The argument of the attach commands is a single string containing
the portmonitor configuration, e.g. its \c type (\c dll, \c lua, ...) and its
\c file. Attaching a new portmonitor replaces the one previously attached (if
any). The reply is \c ok on success, \c fail otherwise.

These commands are defined in the thrift service \c PortCoreMsgs
(\c src/libYARP_os/src/idl/PortCoreMsgs.thrift). From code, they can be sent
using the corresponding methods of yarp::os::impl::PortCoreClient:
\code
yarp::os::impl::PortCoreClient client("/port_name");
client.attachPortmonitorIn("(type dll) (file test_numbers1)");
client.detachPortmonitorIn();
\endcode


\section port_admin_portmonitor_params setPortmonitorParamsIn / setPortmonitorParamsOut / getPortmonitorParamsIn / getPortmonitorParamsOut

\verbatim
setPortmonitorParamsIn  "(key1 value1) (key2 value2)"
setPortmonitorParamsOut "(key1 value1) (key2 value2)"
getPortmonitorParamsIn
getPortmonitorParamsOut
\endverbatim

Set or get the parameters of the portmonitor plug-in attached to the input or
to the output of the port (see \ref port_admin_portmonitor). The parameters are
a single string in yarp::os::Property format; their meaning depends on the
portmonitor (they are passed to its \c setparam / \c getparam methods).

The reply of the set commands is <tt>ok success message</tt>; the reply of the
get commands is <tt>ok success params message</tt>, where \c params is a string
in yarp::os::Property format. \c success is \c fail if no portmonitor is
attached to the port, and in this case \c message describes the error.

For example, the \c test_numbers1 portmonitor (which adds a value to each
number it receives) accepts the parameter \c increment:
\verbatim
echo "setPortmonitorParamsIn \"(increment 5)\"" | yarp admin rpc /port_name
echo "getPortmonitorParamsIn" | yarp admin rpc /port_name
\endverbatim


\section port_admin_connection_params setConnectionParamsIn / setConnectionParamsOut / getConnectionParamsIn / getConnectionParamsOut

\verbatim
setConnectionParamsIn  /portname "(key1 value1) (key2 value2)"
setConnectionParamsOut /portname "(key1 value1) (key2 value2)"
getConnectionParamsIn  /portname
getConnectionParamsOut /portname
\endverbatim

Set or get the carrier parameters of the connection coming from (\c In) or
going to (\c Out) the specified port. The parameters are a single string in
yarp::os::Property format, and they are passed to the carrier of the
connection (see yarp::os::Carrier::setCarrierParams and
yarp::os::Carrier::getCarrierParams); their meaning depends on the carrier.
For instance, on a connection using the portmonitor carrier
(e.g. <tt>fast_tcp+recv.portmonitor+type.dll+file.test_numbers1</tt>) they
are forwarded to the portmonitor of the connection.

The replies have the same format of the portmonitor parameters commands:
<tt>ok success message</tt> for the set commands, and
<tt>ok success params message</tt> for the get commands. \c success is \c fail
if there is no such connection.

From code, use the corresponding methods of yarp::os::impl::PortCoreClient,
which return the structs yarp::os::return_setPortmonitorParams,
yarp::os::return_getPortmonitorParams, yarp::os::return_setConnectionParams
and yarp::os::return_getConnectionParams. The field \c ret of the returned
struct is \c false if the remote port could not be reached.
\code
yarp::os::impl::PortCoreClient client("/port_name");
client.setPortmonitorParamsIn("(increment 5)");
yarp::os::return_getConnectionParams result = client.getConnectionParamsIn("/other_port");
if (result.ret && result.success) {
    yarp::os::Property params;
    params.fromString(result.params);
}
\endcode


\section port_admin_info getPortInfo

\verbatim
getPortInfo
\endverbatim

Give information about the port itself, and about the process which owns it.
The reply contains, in this order, the fields of:
- yarp::os::ProcessInfoData: name, arguments, scheduling policy and priority,
  and pid of the process;
- yarp::os::PlatformInfoData: name, distribution, release, codename, kernel
  and environment variables of the operating system;
- yarp::os::ThreadInfoData: tid, priority and policy of the main thread of the
  port;
- yarp::os::PortInfoData: whether the port is an input, an output, an rpc
  port, its type and its hostname.

From code, use the corresponding method of yarp::os::impl::PortCoreClient,
which returns the struct yarp::os::return_getPortInfo. The field \c ret of the
returned struct is \c false if the remote port could not be reached.
\code
yarp::os::impl::PortCoreClient client("/port_name");
yarp::os::return_getPortInfo info = client.getPortInfo();
if (info.ret) {
    yInfo() << info.process.name << info.process.pid << info.port.hostname;
}
\endcode


\section port_admin_qos getConnectionQosIn / getConnectionQosOut / setConnectionQosIn / setConnectionQosOut / setProcessScheduling

\verbatim
getConnectionQosIn   /portname
getConnectionQosOut  /portname
setConnectionQosIn   /portname scheduler_priority scheduler_policy qos_tos
setConnectionQosOut  /portname scheduler_priority scheduler_policy qos_tos
setProcessScheduling priority policy
\endverbatim

The quality of service of a connection consists of the scheduling parameters
(priority and policy) of the thread which handles the connection, and of the
type of service (TOS) of the IP packets of the connection.
See \ref channelprioritization for the meaning of the values.

\c getConnectionQosIn and \c getConnectionQosOut give the qos of the
connection coming from (or going to) the specified port. The reply is
<tt>ok found tid scheduler_priority scheduler_policy qos_tos</tt>, where
\c found is \c fail if there is no such connection, \c tid is the id of the
thread which handles the connection, and \c qos_tos is -1 if it is not
available.

\c setConnectionQosIn and \c setConnectionQosOut set the qos of the connection
coming from (or going to) the specified port. A \c scheduler_priority of
-1 leaves the scheduling of the thread unchanged, while a negative \c qos_tos
leaves the type of service unchanged. The values are numeric: see
\ref channelprioritization for the TOS values of the priority levels and of
the DSCP classes. The reply is
<tt>ok success message</tt>, where \c success is \c fail if there is no such
connection or if the qos could not be set, and \c message describes the error.

\c setProcessScheduling sets the scheduling parameters of all the threads of
the process which owns the port. The reply is \c ok on success, \c fail
otherwise.

From code, use the corresponding methods of yarp::os::impl::PortCoreClient,
which return the structs yarp::os::return_getConnectionQos and
yarp::os::return_setConnectionQos, or the higher level functions
yarp::os::Network::getConnectionQos and yarp::os::Network::setConnectionQos,
which configure both sides of a connection. The field \c ret of the returned
struct is \c false if the remote port could not be reached.

*/
